        "factual_summary_text": "The material introduces the Variational Autoencoder (VAE) as a generative model that learns a probabilistic mapping between data x and latent variables z. Training maximizes the Evidence Lower Bound (ELBO), which decomposes into two terms: a reconstruction loss that encourages the decoder p(x|z) to reproduce the input, and a KL divergence that regularizes the approximate posterior q(z|x) toward a prior p(z), typically a standard normal distribution. The ELBO is expressed as L(\u03b8,\u03d5;x)=E_{q(z|x)}[log\u202fp(x|z)]\u2212D_{KL}(q(z|x)\u2016p(z)).\n\nTo enable gradient\u2011based optimization despite the stochastic sampling of z, the reparameterization trick is employed: a latent sample is written as z = \u03bc + \u03c3\u00b7\u03b5, where \u03b5\u223cN(0, I) and \u03bc,\u03c3 are outputs of the encoder network. In TensorFlow this is implemented as z = mu + tf.exp(0.5 * log_var) * eps. The loss computation combines the binary cross\u2011entropy reconstruction term with the analytic KL term for Gaussian q(z|x), yielding a total loss that is back\u2011propagated to update both encoder (\u03d5) and decoder (\u03b8) parameters."
